# Per-user env loaded by direnv (see .envrc) and `cargo xtask`
# (via the RIO_-prefixed env layer). Copy to .env.local and edit. Gitignored.
AWS_PROFILE=beme_sandbox

# k8s provider for `cargo xtask k8s ...`. If set, you can omit -p.
# Valid: k3s (local+Rook), eks (AWS).
RIO_K8S_PROVIDER=k3s

# Per-user EKS cluster name. The S3 backend uses tofu workspaces
# for isolation, but cluster_name is a plain var that defaults to
# "rio-build" — the shared default-workspace cluster. Set this to
# rio-<you> when working in a personal workspace, or `tofu apply`
# will try to rename (= destroy + recreate) the cluster and collide
# with rio-build's account-global IAM role/policy names.
# TF_VAR_cluster_name=rio-yourname
#
# Also select a matching tofu workspace ONCE (not via TF_WORKSPACE
# in .env.local — that env var would also leak into the bootstrap
# state, which is account-global and must stay in `default`):
#   tofu -chdir=infra/eks workspace new yourname
# It persists in infra/eks/.terraform/environment.

# State bucket — defaults to rio-tfstate-${account_id} (computed
# from `aws sts get-caller-identity`). Override if you used a
# custom name or the pattern doesn't hold for your account.
# RIO_TFSTATE_BUCKET=my-custom-tfstate-bucket

# State bucket region — defaults to us-east-2.
# RIO_TFSTATE_REGION=us-east-2

# SSH public key for gateway authorized_keys. Used by both
# `cargo xtask k8s up -p k3s` (local k3s) and `cargo xtask k8s deploy -p eks`.
# The key's comment is STRIPPED → single-tenant mode (builds work
# with zero tenant setup). Default: ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
# RIO_SSH_PUBKEY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

# If set, use this as the authorized_keys comment instead of stripping.
# The gateway maps comment → tenant_name; the scheduler requires that
# tenant to exist. Create it first:
#   kubectl -n rio-system exec deploy/rio-scheduler -- rio-cli create-tenant <name>
# RIO_SSH_TENANT=my-team

# kache (cross-worktree cargo build cache) knobs. These three are the
# supported per-user overrides — the devshell shellHook also loads them
# from this file for non-direnv `nix develop` entries, so keep them as
# single-line VAR=value entries with PLAIN values (no quotes, spaces,
# or escapes: direnv and xtask's dotenvy would strip quotes, a raw
# shell export would keep them, so the loader rejects anything outside
# [A-Za-z0-9_/.+-] with a warning rather than let readers disagree).

# Disable caching. Value-based: 1 or true disables (kache's own parse:
# exactly "1" or any-ASCII-case "true"; "0"/"false"/empty do NOT
# disable). Also the right knob for prolonged DEGRADED sqlx sessions
# (rio-buildhash warns about unkeyed/uncacheable builds): those builds
# still write never-replayable entries into the store on every compile.
# Toggling is free — verified: current cargo does not fingerprint the
# wrapper, and the wrapper stays in place regardless, so no rebuilds
# either way. Disabled rebuilds self-heal past earlier kache restores:
# the wrapper sweeps THIS compile's own restores (anchored by the
# unit's -C extra-filename) out of its --out-dir before exec'ing the
# real rustc — sibling units' and other configs' restores are
# untouched. If "output file … is not writeable" still appears, that
# is NOT proof the spawner bypassed the wrapper — two wrapper-INTERNAL
# fallbacks also skip the failing file: (1) a non-cargo-shaped
# -C extra-filename (missing, or not exactly 16 lowercase hex) drops
# the sweep to its conservative shared-inode arm, which deliberately
# must not touch nlink-1 reflink restores; (2) an unrecognized bare
# argv[1] (custom RUSTC word, [build] rustc bare word) leaves every
# bypass arm dead — the wrapper prints a "not a recognized compiler"
# warning to stderr in that case, so grep your build log for it
# before concluding bare-cargo bypass (bare cargo without
# RUSTC_WRAPPER is the remaining explanation). Either way: delete the
# file or `cargo clean -p <crate>`; never chmod it writable: a
# hardlinked restore's inode is SHARED with the store, so rustc would
# truncate the store blob in place and poison future restores.
# Cost of staying disabled for weeks: nothing stamps the default
# store epoch while disabled (by design, so an opted-out store can
# age out) — after 14 idle days the entry-time GC prunes it and
# re-enabling is a one-time cold rebuild. Mitigation: re-enable
# occasionally, or set RIO_KACHE_CACHE_DIR — a configured store is
# re-stamped at every shell entry even while disabled.
# KACHE_DISABLED=1

# Override the store eviction cap. The devshell config pins 256GiB
# (kache's own default would be 50GiB). Rio-namespaced like the store
# knob: KACHE_MAX_SIZE is kache's own variable, so a machine-global
# export from another kache-using project must not shrink OUR eviction
# budget — the wrapper ignores it and honors only this one. Accepted
# shape: <integer> immediately followed by KiB|MiB|GiB|TiB (anything
# else is ignored with a wrapper warning).
# Eviction runs in the kache daemon, which snapshots config at spawn —
# run `kache daemon stop` once after changing this (it auto-respawns).
# RIO_KACHE_MAX_SIZE=512GiB

# Relocate the store (default: ~/.cache/rio-build/kache/<env-salt>).
# Useful if your home cache lives on a different filesystem than your
# worktrees — same-FS enables reflink/hardlink restores instead of
# copies. Rio-namespaced on purpose: the wrapper deliberately ignores
# kache's own KACHE_CACHE_DIR so a machine-global export from another
# kache-using project can never repoint this repo's store. Notes:
# overriding bypasses the env-salt epoch (after a toolchain bump,
# prefer wiping the store over debugging stale-binary ENOENTs).
# The value is CANONICALIZED (symlinks, //, /./ resolved) before use,
# so keep-alive and the prune-skip track the real location no matter
# how the path is spelled — kache also sees the canonical spelling.
# Flip side: if the value traverses a symlink you later retarget, the
# resolved location updates at the next shell entry / compile, not
# mid-session. A value equal to or ABOVE ~/.cache/rio-build/kache
# disables epoch pruning for that session with a warning (every epoch
# would be inside your configured store; the prune never deletes
# inside one) — configure a subdirectory instead, e.g.
# ~/.cache/rio-build/kache/mystore.
# Pruning: a store relocated OUTSIDE ~/.cache/rio-build/kache is never
# pruned (yours to manage). One parked UNDER that root is kept live
# WHILE CONFIGURED — configuring the path is the keep-alive: the
# devshell re-stamps it AND its top-level epoch dir at every entry
# (even with KACHE_DISABLED set), the wrapper re-stamps both on every
# enabled compile, and the entry-time prune skips any epoch dir that
# is a path-prefix of the configured store. It is pruned only after
# 14 genuinely idle days (no shell entry with this config, no enabled
# compile) and only if its dirname is 12-hex salt-shaped. The 14-day
# pruning of OLD epochs under that root runs at shell entry either
# way, so pre-relocation epochs age out instead of leaking 256GiB
# dirs.
# RIO_KACHE_CACHE_DIR=/fast-disk/rio-kache
